# Item-slot indicator effects

Source proposal: the 2026-10-03 conversation proposing optional provider-registered effects, reusable deformable geometry, renderer-owned shader resources, and shared frame inputs.
Status: Phases 1 and 2 complete after their required verification, second review, and independent completion audits. GPU resources, animation, effect rendering, and runtime validation remain outstanding.

## Goal and scope

Allow an indicator provider registration to attach an optional rendering effect while preserving provider selection and cached fill/color sampling. Animate actual mesh silhouettes using reusable geometry and vertex shaders by default. Keep ItemSlotIndicatorSystem responsible for composition and selection, with rendering resource and animation responsibilities in separate owners.

Existing registrations retain their rectangular presentation. One minimal deformation effect verifies the extension boundary; selecting production themes for water, freshness, clothing, or other items is separate work.

Out of scope: arbitrary geometry-stage support, generated geometry per frame, per-item CPU simulation, effect stacking, public third-party extension compatibility, and redesigning provider priority or refresh policies. Do not introduce batching that changes GUI draw order. GPU execution alone is not a performance guarantee.

## Assumptions and implementation questions

- The effect attaches to the winning registration; the provider initially continues returning ItemSlotIndicator(Fill, Color). Per-item effect selection and dynamic provider-specific parameter payloads are deferred.
- Effect definitions describe immutable assets, geometry requirements, parameters, and motion requirements; they never own live GPU objects.
- The contracts below establish the client frame callback, camera source, GUI transform, shader lifecycle, subdivision limits, shader inputs, and performance gates for the inspected engine version. Recheck affected integration points after an engine update.
- Live appearance and GPU timing require user-run acceptance. Headless tests cannot establish either; do not launch the game for visual acceptance.

## Phase 1: Establish rendering and compatibility contracts

Outcome: implementation has verified engine integration points and an explicit effect contract.

    ✔ Inspect ItemSlotIndicatorSystem, ItemSlotIndicatorRegistration, ItemSlotIndicatorRenderer, ItemSlotIndicatorPatch, existing providers, and their selection/scheduling tests.
    ✔ Verify the installed engine path used by the item-slot GUI hook, including GUI matrices, slot scaling, depth, blending, clipping, active shader, and item draw ordering.
    ✔ Identify a once-per-frame update boundary and authoritative camera orientation source; verify how repeated slot draws and multiple GUI surfaces share one frame snapshot.
    ✔ Verify engine shader registration, compilation, reload invalidation, and disposal behavior before defining resource replacement ownership.
    ✔ Define the common mesh/shader contract: normalized horizontal coordinate, bottom/surface edge identity, slot bounds, GUI transform, clamped fill, straight-alpha input color, time, motion, and immutable effect parameters.
    ✔ Define output constraints: slot containment, bottom anchoring, premultiplied output, zero-fill invisibility, full-fill coverage, and behavior near fill limits. Deformation must not turn empty/full values into misleading resource levels.
    ✔ Define finite-value handling, bounded subdivision counts, supported topology, parameter validation, and effect ID collision behavior.
    ✔ Establish a matched performance comparison workload and acceptance criteria, including visible slot counts, GUI scale, effect mix, CPU submission time, and GPU time.

Completion criteria:
    ✔ Integration points and unresolved engine assumptions are recorded in this plan or the owning API documentation, with source references.
    ✔ Input/output and lifetime contracts are precise enough to implement without choosing production visual themes.

### Established contracts and source evidence (2026-10-03)

Evidence boundary: source and installed-assembly IL inspection only, using Mono.Cecil without loading a graphics context. No build, test execution, game launch, shader compilation, appearance check, or GPU timing is claimed by this investigation. Numerical limits below are chosen implementation contracts, not measured optimal settings.

Installed engine: G:/Vintagestory/VintagestoryLib.dll, assembly version 1.22.7.0, SHA256 E08F22B493B92FEAF0AAEB79D22437EA0F7EFC38AA7F72A04A47F98BC0E40DF0; paired G:/Vintagestory/VintagestoryAPI.dll SHA256 034283E7E9D98EAE45EE63005576FD89BADC3C995B531CC4C3FE46F3EB2D3296. Assembly type/method references below are reproducible inspection anchors rather than claims based only on API comments.

#### Selection and compatibility

- Source: ../VanillaExpanded/src/ItemSlotIndicators/{ItemSlotIndicatorSystem,ItemSlotIndicatorRegistration,ItemSlotIndicatorRenderer,ItemSlotIndicatorPatch,AdaptiveSamplingOptions}.cs. Selection is descending priority with stable equal-priority order; zero fill is an applicable result. Caching belongs to each registration and weak stack identity, with slot/inventory/API/collectible/configuration invalidation. Default interval is 1,000 ms; adaptive registrations use 100 ms while active and 1,000 ms settling. Preserve these policies independently of animation.
- Source: ../VanillaExpanded/src/{PerishableItemSlots/FreshnessIndicatorProvider,PreparationIndicators/PreparationIndicatorProvider,ClothingIndicators/ClothingIndicatorProvider,LiquidContainerIndicators/LiquidContainerIndicatorProvider,WateringCanIndicators/WateringCanIndicatorProvider,NightVisionIndicators/NightVisionFuelIndicatorProvider}.cs. Providers return presentation fill, not necessarily physical resource fraction: empty watering cans, exhausted night-vision devices, and zero-condition clothing deliberately return Fill=1 for warning overlays. Preserve those results. Zero-fill invisibility applies to the returned indicator value; never infer emptiness again in rendering.
- Verification sources inspected: ../VanillaExpanded.Tests/Unit/ItemSlotIndicators/{ItemSlotIndicatorTests,ItemSlotIndicatorSchedulingTests,AdaptiveSamplingTests}.cs cover priority/ties/zero fill, bounds/premultiplication, cache expiry/identity mutation, independent schedules, threshold handling, and settling. Their assertions define existing regression obligations; inspection is not a fresh passing test run.

#### GUI integration and render-state boundary

- Source: ../../vsapi/Client/UI/Elements/Impl/Interactive/Inventory/GuiElementItemSlotGridBase.cs, RenderInteractiveElements. Installed API IL confirms scaled slot size and the dt-bearing slot overload at IL_02ff, bracketed by PushScissor at IL_02b3 and PopScissor at IL_030f. Background/highlight precede this call; item content and subsequent overlays retain engine order. Keep the current interception site and every forwarded argument.
- Installed Lib: RenderAPIGame.RenderItemstackToGui delegates to InventoryItemRenderer.RenderItemstackToGui. The latter owns item transforms and callbacks. Indicator rendering must complete before that delegation; do not query item rendering a second time.
- Installed Lib: ClientMain.RenderToDefaultFramebuffer enters OrthoMode and triggers Ortho once for enabled overlays. OrthoMode uses downward screen Y and an existing model-view Z translation. GuiManager.OnRenderFrameGUI additionally translates Z between dialogs. Use IRenderAPI.CurrentProjectionMatrix and CurrentModelviewMatrix at each draw, not a recreated screen projection or one matrix captured for all dialogs. API reference: ../../vsapi/Client/API/IRenderAPI.cs.
- Keep indicator local Z=80, with the slot item at its unchanged incoming Z (normally 90). Slot side S=GuiElement.scaled(GuiElementPassiveItemSlot.unscaledSlotSize), independent of item model size. Use the incoming center including engine notification jitter. The legacy rectangle computes bottom-aligned fill then the engine's float Render2DTexturePremultipliedAlpha truncates X/Y to integers. Preserve those default/fallback appearance calculations; effect geometry may use subpixel coordinates and needs edge acceptance at fractional GUI scales.
- Installed Lib: RenderAPIGame.Render2DTexturePremultipliedAlpha enables premultiplied blending, calls ClientMain.Render2DTexture, then selects standard blending. ClientMain.Render2DTexture modifies the active GUI shader's uniforms and temporarily pushes/transforms/pops model-view. It does not select a custom effect program. Use RenderMesh for effects.
- Installed Lib: ShaderProgramBase.Use rejects switching directly from another active program; Stop clears engine tracking and unbinds that program's samplers/UBOs. Capture CurrentActiveShader, Stop it, Use the effect, then Stop the effect and Use the saved live program in finally. Do not bypass engine shader tracking with only GL.UseProgram. Effects use no samplers, UBOs, world-shader includes, or textures initially, so they do not alter shared GUI uniform values or texture bindings. Normal GUI draw sites establish their own GUI uniforms; the custom path must not overwrite them.
- Stop/Use is not a complete resource-state restore for arbitrary saved shaders: Stop unbinds custom samplers, while Use can write uniforms and bind textures for world-shader includes. The supported hook is the engine GUI pass. Verify its saved program is the current engine GUI program; on an unexpected/null program skip only the indicator and always forward the original item call. This unsupported invocation is distinct from an unavailable effect in the supported GUI pass, which must render the rectangle fallback. Do not switch arbitrary world/custom shaders in this feature.
- For the supported GUI switch, capture/restore lightPosition as well: ShaderProgramBase.Use writes it even when returning to the same GUI program. Also capture/restore the GUI program's affected indexed sampler/UBO bindings (including the Animation UBO binding declared by gui.vsh), with UBO buffer/range offset/size and the generic UniformBuffer binding, because Stop/Use can modify those. Restore indexed UBO bindings first and generic binding last. Cache discovery of affected indices per program identity, not live binding values. Restore GUI uniform and resource bindings after Use, not before it.
- Draw with depth testing enabled, inherited depth function, depth writes disabled, blend enabled, additive blend equations and premultiplied factors ONE / ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA for RGB and alpha. Disable culling for the strip. Preserve inherited scissor and stencil without changing them; they continue to clip every effect.
- Snapshot/restore the states actually touched: depth-test enable and write mask; cull enable; blend enable, separate RGB/alpha equations and factors (including affected indexed draw-buffer state if using engine blend helpers); active shader; VAO and its element-buffer binding. Installed ClientPlatformWindows.RenderMesh binds a VAO/element buffer and leaves zero bound, so restoring shader alone is insufficient. Prefer setting only draw-buffer 0 blending for the single GUI color output to avoid collateral indexed-state changes. Leave framebuffer, viewport, depth range/function, color mask, scissor/stencil, and engine matrix stacks untouched. Restore all touched state on exceptions before any fallback draw; do not swallow exceptions from the subsequent engine item draw.
- The rectangle helper also changes GUI uniforms and texture-unit state, and ClientMain.Render2DTexture only pops its matrix on normal return. Therefore implement the fallback through a narrow GUI rectangle adapter with equivalent geometry/rounding/color behavior: construct its transformed model-view in owned scratch storage and use the engine GUI program plus an owned standard GUI quad/white texture directly, without touching engine matrix stacks. The GUI quad uses QuadMeshUtil.GetQuad (positions [-1,1], UVs, six indices), a distinct cache layout from the effect ABI; source ../../vsapi/Client/Model/Mesh/QuadMeshUtil.cs. Compose current model-view * Translate(truncatedX,truncatedY,80) * Scale(width,height,0) * Scale(0.5,0.5,0) * Translate(1,1,0), matching the helper.
- Capture/restore GUI uniform values the adapter changes (rgbaIn, extraGlow, applyColor, noTexture, tex2d, overlayOpacity, normalShaded, projectionMatrix, modelViewMatrix), active texture unit, and unit 0's texture/sampler bindings, plus the common state scope. Exact names and unit 0 were verified in ShaderProgramGui setters and G:/Vintagestory/assets/game/shaders/gui.{vsh,fsh}. Bind the owned white texture without altering its wrap parameters during draws. Preserve other GUI uniforms as the helper does; use engine APIs for shader tracking. Use preallocated storage, and include restoration cost in the disabled-effects performance comparison. An engine-owned GUI program failure is outside effect-fallback guarantees; no indicator implementation can recover an unusable host GUI renderer.
- Existing ItemSlotIndicatorRenderer finally forces depth writes/test on rather than restoring captured values. The later renderer integration must apply the state boundary to both custom and rectangular rendering; this is scoped render-state correction, not a claim that the current renderer already restores arbitrary callers. The fallback adapter replaces the unsafe helper invocation, not the rectangle presentation.

#### Shared frame snapshot and camera source

- Installed Lib: PlayerCamera registers OnBeforeRenderFrame3D at Before order 0; Camera.Update constructs CameraMatrixOrigin and applies roll. RenderAPIGame.CameraMatrixOrigin returns MainCamera.CameraMatrixOrigin. This is the authoritative rendered orientation source; player entity yaw alone can miss camera-mode offsets.
- Installed Lib: GuiManager.OnServerIdentificationReceived registers OnRenderFrameGUI at Ortho order 1. ClientEventManager.RegisterRenderer inserts in render-order order, and TriggerRenderStage invokes each entry once. Register one system-owned IRenderer at Ortho order 0.99, before GuiManager. It only updates state, draws nothing, and is unregistered at disposal. All normal inventory/hotbar slot draws in that Ortho pass read its immutable snapshot. No slot draw advances time or motion. When overlays are skipped, no snapshot advances; an out-of-pass/custom invocation uses the latest snapshot or neutral initial state and never creates a new frame.
- Read/copy the rotation basis from CameraMatrixOrigin, ignoring translation; do not retain an engine-owned mutable array. A narrow adapter obtains public ClientMain.MainCamera identity and its nonpublic Camera.CameraMode via a cached field accessor for reset detection (visibility verified in installed metadata). World/player/camera identity or actual mode changes reset motion. Keep these engine-specific accesses outside the pure frame-state math.
- Motion contract: relative rotation between consecutive orthonormal camera bases, expressed in previous camera-local axes. X input is rightward look rate (about local up), Y input is upward look rate (about local right with sign chosen accordingly), in radians/second; ignore roll for the two output channels. Use shortest-arc relative rotation, avoiding Euler wrapping and pole singularities. Validate right/up signs with controlled rotations. Normalize each rate by 4 radians/second, clamp to [-1,1], then exponentially smooth with weight 1-exp(-dt/0.12). Effects decide how to interpret this bounded shared signal; no per-slot spring state.
- Use monotonic elapsed time, not world/calendar time or the per-item dt argument. First sample, invalid/non-orthonormal basis, nonpositive dt, gap >0.25 s, camera/world/mode change, or relative turn >pi/2 resets motion and establishes a fresh baseline. No catch-up. Validate unit/orthogonal basis within 0.001 and determinant near +1; invalid/missing camera yields neutral motion but does not stop time-only effects. Reactivation after no motion subscribers also starts neutral.
- Animation time contract: accumulate finite accepted dt in double on the CPU, publish seconds modulo 64 as a float. A long/invalid gap contributes zero elapsed animation time; normal GUI frames continue animation even when world simulation is paused. Shader time expressions must be periodic over 64 seconds (integer cycle counts per 64 seconds), preventing a visible wrap. This intentionally bounds initial periodic effects; arbitrary long-running/nonperiodic simulations are outside scope. Phase 4 implements and tests these decisions.

#### Definition, mesh, and shader ABI

- Definition is an immutable value: canonical lower-case namespaced effect ID, file shader asset domain and shared basename for .vsh/.fsh, ABI version=1, topology, subdivision count, one finite Vector4 parameter block, and NeedsCameraMotion. Copy values; do not hold mutable arrays or provider callbacks. Each effect documents/validates its parameter block's units/ranges; unused lanes are zero. Unknown ABI/topology, invalid names, out-of-range counts, and nonfinite parameters fail registration before mutation.
- Same canonical ID with an identical complete definition shares a resource entry; a differing definition under that ID throws an argument error and leaves registrations unchanged. Parameter variants therefore require distinct effect IDs initially. No silent replacement. Use a globally unique engine shader basename prefixed vanillaexpanded_itemslot_; shader assets use that basename in the declared domain. Resolve each effect ID to a program key (domain, basename, ABI). Distinct effect IDs may reuse the same program key with their own per-draw parameters/geometry; do not register or compile it twice. Reject the same engine registration name with a differing program key, because ShaderRegistry replaces entries by name. This avoids duplicating shader files for parameter variants. Missing/broken assets are runtime availability failures, not priority changes.
- Geometry is an indexed triangle list, not a geometry-shader-generated strip. Quad is N=1; deformable fill strip permits N=2..64 equal-width segments (default 16). Vertices=2(N+1), indices=6N, maximum 130 vertices/384 indices. No dynamic subdivision. Cache key is ABI/topology/N, independent of slot size, fill, and effect ID.
- Use MeshData.xyz only for the position attribute at location 0: vec3(u,edge,0), u=i/N, edge=0 bottom or 1 surface. No UV/color buffer is required. Emit two consistently wound triangles per segment. API source: ../../vsapi/Client/Model/Mesh/MeshData.cs; existing shader location convention: ../VanillaExpanded/assets/vanillaexpanded/shaders/radial_menu.vsh. Disable culling during indicator draws so screen-Y inversion is harmless.
- Common uniforms: projectionMatrix and modelViewMatrix (mat4, current GUI matrices); slotBounds (vec4, left/top/width/height in scaled pixels); fill (float); color (vec4 straight RGBA); timeSeconds (float [0,64)); motion (vec2 [-1,1]); effectParameters (vec4); segmentCount (int). Shader wrapper uses local Z=80. Optimized-out optional uniforms are legal; bind them only if present. Required position/transform/fill contract must be checked when preparing a program.
- Coordinates: x=left+u*S, y=top+S*(1-edge*h(u)), gl_Position=projectionMatrix*modelViewMatrix*vec4(x,y,80,1). No shader may change u, the bottom edge, slot extent, clip depth, or framebuffer; fragment shaders emit only color attachment 0, with no gl_FragDepth or side effects.
- h(u)=fill+d(u); require |d(u)| <= min(0.1, 0.25*fill, 0.25*(1-fill)), with zero deformation at fill 0/1. Require the discrete trapezoidal mean of d over the N segments to be zero, so the triangulated area still represents fill. Use analytically zero-mean sampled wave/tilt functions or subtract their analytically known discrete mean; do not clamp individual heights after deformation because that biases area. The bound ensures heights stay in [0,1]. Trusted effect assets must prove these conditions with mathematical and sampled boundary checks; this is not a sandbox for arbitrary shaders.
- CPU input sanitation: nonfinite fill becomes zero, otherwise clamp [0,1]; nonfinite color component becomes zero, otherwise clamp [0,1]. Nonfinite slot coordinates/dimensions, nonpositive width/height, or invalid matrices skip the draw; zero/negative left/top coordinates remain valid for partially clipped slots. Fill=0 or alpha=0 skips submission while retaining the selected provider. Fill=1 is full coverage, including provider-defined warnings. Fragment output is vec4(rgb*alpha,alpha), exactly one premultiplication; initial effects preserve provider opacity and color. Future coloration contracts require an explicit extension.

#### Program and mesh lifetime

- API sources: ../../vsapi/Client/API/IShaderAPI.cs and ../../vsapi/Client/Render/IShaderProgram.cs. Installed Lib: ShaderAPI.RegisterFileShaderProgram sets LoadFromFile and calls ShaderRegistry.RegisterShaderProgram; registration assigns/reuses PassId by name and loads source, but callers still invoke Compile. ShaderProgram.Compile checks version, compiles stages and links through engine ownership. Use engine-created objects and engine compilation, never an independent raw-GL compiler.
- Installed Lib: ShaderRegistry.ReloadShaders disposes/nulls registered programs and rebuilds defaults. ShaderAPI.ReloadShaders subsequently invokes ClientEventManager.TriggerReloadShaders. Recreate/re-register each custom effect during that event and compile once; invalidate all old handles first. Do not retain an old program as a reload fallback. Mesh geometry survives shader reload because its ABI is fixed; rebuilding meshes is only necessary on disposal/reinitialization or a future ABI change.
- ShaderProgramBase.Dispose is guarded/idempotent. The cache owns its references, but the engine registry also disposes registered programs on reload. Cleanup must tolerate that shared lifecycle, release partial creations once, and never dispose another effect/default engine program. The public API has no unregister operation: dispose/drop owned references at shutdown, reuse stable registration names after reload, and never grow fresh names per attempt. Registering a name replaces its slot without disposing the former object, so explicit replacement outside a global reload must dispose the formerly owned object.
- Prepare resources at client initialization or an explicit render-thread registration/reload boundary. A registration arriving during iteration is queued for the next preparation boundary; it renders plain until prepared. Do not compile in TryGetIndicator or Render. Missing/failed programs remain unavailable until the next explicit attempt; log each effect once per attempt. Only the selected effect falls back. Normal shader failure cannot suppress the subsequent item draw.

#### Performance acceptance protocol

- Compare the unchanged rectangle baseline against the new renderer with effects disabled and with the minimal deformation effect. Release build, same machine/GPU/driver, window resolution, GUI scale, scene, item stacks/fills/colors, provider schedules, camera trace, and frame cap/VSync setting; record these conditions. Use 10, 100, and 250 actually visible slots at GUI scale 1.0 and 1.5, with enough viewport area to avoid comparing different clipping counts. If a workload cannot fit, enlarge the viewport consistently for both variants and record it.
- For each size/scale use fixed fills cycling 0/0.01/0.25/0.5/0.99/1 and unchanged colors. Test no effects, one shared effect on all eligible slots, and an alternating plain/effect mix. Test static and repeated scripted camera input. Also test N=64 as a bounded worst case; N=16 is the default comparison. Compile and upload before timing, warm up for 10 seconds, then collect three alternating paired 30-second runs per case.
- CPU: measure aggregate indicator selection/submission and shared frame-update time per frame separately, reporting median/p95 and paired differences. GPU: use asynchronous timestamp pairs around the same indicator draw regions, sum disjoint regions only, read results several frames later without waiting/GL.Finish. Keep query overhead identical in both variants and report timer resolution/measurement overhead. Whole-frame time is a separate sanity measurement, not a sum of nested CPU scopes.
- Chosen acceptance budgets at every matched workload: effects-disabled median/p95 CPU and GPU regression <=0.05 ms each; enabled effects (including N=64) add <=0.25 ms median and <=0.5 ms p95 on CPU and GPU independently. Use the median of the three per-run paired differences for each statistic. If run spread/timer resolution prevents deciding the threshold, report inconclusive and repeat under controlled conditions; never round uncertainty into a pass. These are initial project gates, not claims of universal device performance.
- Structural gates: zero steady-state shader compiles, mesh uploads, and per-slot animation allocations; frame update once per GUI frame; provider invocation counts match the rectangle workload; one indicator draw per nonzero visible result at most. Record shader switches and resource counts, confirm bounded counts across reloads, and keep clipped/zero-fill skips equivalent. Runtime visual/lifecycle acceptance remains Phase 6 work.

Traceability for Phase 1: compatibility task -> selection/provider/test sources above -> preserve presentation and scheduling -> source inspection; installed-render/frame/lifecycle tasks -> named installed methods plus API sources above -> verified hook/order/ownership -> IL inspection; ABI/output/validation tasks -> conversation architecture and established contracts above -> bounded geometry and precise semantics -> design review; performance task -> protocol above -> matched measurable gates -> protocol review, execution deferred to Phase 6.

Remaining evidence boundary: no required engine integration assumption is intentionally left open for this inspected version. Graphics-context correctness, shader compilation, visual quality, failure/reload behavior under execution, and performance budgets still require the later implementation and acceptance work; static inspection does not satisfy those later gates.

## Phase 2: Attach effects to provider selection

Depends on Phase 1.
Outcome: selection carries an optional effect without changing provider behavior.

    ✔ Add an immutable ItemSlotIndicatorEffectDefinition with stable ID, shader asset references, supported geometry description, bounded subdivision count, parameters, and motion-input requirement.
    ✔ Extend registration with an optional effect definition; existing registration calls remain valid and default to the plain rectangle.
    ✔ Introduce a render-selection value containing the sampled ItemSlotIndicator and the winning registration's effect definition.
    ✔ Keep IItemSlotIndicatorProvider.TryGetIndicator and its fill/color result unchanged; adapt selection consumers and tests deliberately without adding redundant forwarding APIs.
    ✔ Preserve descending priority, stable equal-priority ordering, absent-provider skipping, and zero-fill results as successful selections.
    ✔ Preserve weak sample ownership, context invalidation, refresh intervals, and adaptive sampling. Time and camera changes must not invalidate provider samples.
    ✔ Verify effect attachment belongs to the selected registration even when the indicator result comes from cache; verify default registrations retain ordinary rendering selection.

Completion criteria:
    ✔ Focused selection and scheduling checks pass, including cached effect selection and zero-fill priority behavior.
    ✔ No provider owns shaders, meshes, rendering calls, or animation updates.

Implementation evidence (2026-10-03): ../VanillaExpanded/src/ItemSlotIndicators/Effects/{ItemSlotIndicatorEffectDefinition,ItemSlotIndicatorTopology}.cs enforce immutable metadata, canonical names, ABI/geometry limits, finite parameters, and identity compatibility before insertion. ItemSlotIndicatorRegistration attaches metadata outside its unchanged sampling state; ItemSlotIndicatorSystem.TryGetRenderSelection carries the winning indicator/effect to the existing hook and renderer. The renderer still uses the ordinary rectangle until GPU resource/rendering work is implemented.

Verification: a fresh Release compilation of the mod and unit-test project succeeded; all 111 tests in Unit.ItemSlotIndicators passed, including definition validation, atomic collision rejection, cached/zero-fill selection, priorities, scheduling, and context/adaptive sampling. Expanded provider checks passed 192 of 196 tests. The four watering-can opacity expectation failures reproduced identically in a freshly built clean HEAD archive (12 passed, 4 failed in that provider suite); their source and tests are unchanged by this work. Existing unrelated compiler warnings remain. Second review and independent completion audit found no outstanding Phase 2 issues. Shader-specific parameter ranges belong to each future effect declaration, while the shared definition enforces finite copied values.

## Phase 3: Own reusable rendering resources

Depends on Phases 1–2.
Outcome: valid effect definitions resolve to shared, lifecycle-managed GPU resources.

    ☐ Add focused owners under ItemSlotIndicators/Rendering for effect programs and reusable meshes; retain one clearly named responsibility per file.
    ☐ Implement reusable quad and subdivided fill-strip geometry. Each strip sample has bottom and surface vertices using the established input contract.
    ☐ Cache meshes by geometry requirements and programs by effect identity; allow multiple provider registrations to share resources without duplicate compilation or uploads.
    ☐ Validate incompatible reuse of an effect ID deterministically; distinguish invalid registration data from runtime shader failure.
    ☐ Register and compile shader assets through the engine-owned shader API on the graphics thread. Prepare resources at initialization/registration or explicit reload boundaries, never in recurring slot draws.
    ☐ Handle reload by rebuilding resources under the verified engine ownership rules. Do not retain invalid program handles or assume engine reload preserves old programs.
    ☐ Isolate failures per effect, log once per load attempt, dispose partial resources, and retry only at an explicit load/reload boundary.
    ☐ Make disposal release each owned resource once and unsubscribe reload callbacks; support partial initialization and repeated cleanup safely.
    ☐ Verify shared-resource identity, failure isolation, and lifecycle behavior with focused checks; reserve actual compilation/link validation for a graphics context.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Resource ownership is explicit and recurring draws require no compilation, mesh generation, or mesh uploads.
    ☐ A failing effect does not invalidate unrelated effects or the existing rectangle resources.

## Phase 4: Supply shared frame inputs

Depends on Phases 1–2; may be developed independently of Phase 3.
Outcome: all indicator draws in a frame consume consistent animation inputs.

    ☐ Add ItemSlotIndicatorFrameState as the owner of animation timing and shared damped camera-motion state.
    ☐ Update at the verified frame boundary, never once per slot query or draw; expose a read-only snapshot for render consumers.
    ☐ Define motion axes, units, normalization, damping, and amplitude bounds; effects interpret the shared signal without introducing per-item CPU simulations.
    ☐ Handle angle wrapping, invalid input, first sample, frame-rate variation, long gaps, unavailable camera, and world/camera replacement without impulses or accumulated catch-up.
    ☐ Define animation clock precision and pause/resume behavior; avoid precision loss or discontinuities from unbounded float time.
    ☐ Avoid camera-dependent work when no registered effect needs it, while ensuring reactivation starts from a fresh baseline.
    ☐ Verify deterministic motion response and reset behavior with controlled inputs; verify repeated draws consume the same snapshot without advancing it.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Animation input production is independent of provider sampling and visible slot count.
    ☐ Missing camera input produces a defined neutral motion signal while time-only animation remains usable.

## Phase 5: Integrate effect rendering and rectangle fallback

Depends on Phases 2–4.
Outcome: an opted-in indicator can animate its silhouette behind the normal item draw.

    ☐ Extend the renderer to resolve the selected effect, bind shared geometry/programs, and submit the common inputs plus immutable effect parameters.
    ☐ Retain the ordinary rectangle path for registrations without effects and for unavailable/failed effects, using the same selected provider's fill and color.
    ☐ Never choose another provider or hide a valid indicator because its effect failed.
    ☐ Keep ItemSlotIndicatorPatch thin: select, render, then forward every original item-render argument unchanged.
    ☐ Preserve the current intended GUI layering, clipping, scale, and bottom alignment. Restore every modified surrounding render state, including active shader and depth-write/blend state, on success and exceptional exits.
    ☐ Apply the established fill/containment contract to effect geometry; avoid transparent fragments writing depth or changing neighboring slot appearance.
    ☐ Add one minimal deformation shader and an explicit development/test opt-in path to prove the boundary, without assigning production themes to existing providers.
    ☐ Verify the shader deforms surface vertices while retaining the bottom boundary; keep fragment shading simple and premultiply output exactly once.
    ☐ Define ownership/removal of any temporary diagnostic registration or instrumentation before shipping; retain reusable validation assets only with a clear purpose.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Default and failure paths retain ordinary indicators and normal item rendering.
    ☐ The demonstration animates from frame inputs while cached provider data remains unchanged.
    ☐ No new per-frame mesh upload, shader compilation, or per-slot motion simulation occurs.

## Phase 6: Validate, review, and document

Depends on Phase 5.
Outcome: implementation evidence and its limits are explicit.

    ☐ Run builds and tests through subagents, as required by repository instructions; run focused indicator/provider regressions and relevant rendering-contract checks.
    ☐ Verify shader assets are included in the distributable and compile/link in the supported engine graphics context; distinguish package inspection from runtime shader validation.
    ☐ Obtain user-run appearance checks for inventory/hotbar placement, GUI scaling, scrolling/clipping, item overlays, empty/full/near-limit fill, camera movement, long gaps, and camera/world changes.
    ☐ Exercise failed effect loading, repeated shader reload, and shutdown; verify fallback, state restoration, bounded diagnostics, and resource cleanup in the graphics context.
    ☐ Measure matched rectangle/effect workloads at the agreed slot counts and GUI scales. Report CPU and GPU measurements separately, including shader switches and mesh-upload counts; do not claim GPU speedups from headless tests.
    ☐ Resolve regressions or explicitly record unmet acceptance criteria; optimize based on measurements without changing GUI draw order.
    ☐ Document provider registration, geometry/shader contracts, frame inputs, resource ownership, failure behavior, and how to add an effect.
    ☐ Ensure new classes/methods have language-appropriate documentation, nontrivial logic comments, and visibility-first method regions. Keep project staging terminology confined to planning documents.
    ☐ Perform a second implementation review and independent audit against this task list before marking completion; reconcile suspected issues against the full contract.

Final acceptance:
    ☐ Optional registration effects, shared geometry/programs, vertex deformation, shared frame inputs, fallback, and lifetime handling are implemented and verified.
    ☐ Existing provider selection, sampling, and unthemed rendering remain compatible.
    ☐ Required runtime evidence and matched performance results meet the established criteria; outstanding user-run checks remain explicitly unchecked.
    ☐ Production effect themes and geometry-stage support remain deferred rather than implicitly claimed as delivered.
